### Technical specification — Client Pickup Ticket Management

Technology: Angular + ASP.NET Core Web API + SQL Server + Dynamics AX 2012 R3 AIF/custom services.

Your application will manage the entire client pickup process for one company and one warehouse. Each client receives one arrival ticket covering multiple sales orders, and the ticket progresses through credit validation, warehouse preparation, and collection.

The key design decision is to keep the pickup workflow in a dedicated application while using AX 2012 R3 as the source of truth for customer accounts, sales orders, balances, and ERP transactions.

### 1\. Overall architecture

Angular frontend

Commercial · Reception · Credit manager · Warehouse · Public queue display

ASP.NET Core Web API

Authentication · Business rules · Workflow · Validation · AX adapter · SignalR

SQL Server

Tickets, plans, history, integration logs

AX 2012 R3 AIF

Customers, orders, credit checks, release, status

### Recommended deployment

* Angular: hosted on IIS, accessible from authorized internal workstations.

* ASP.NET Core API: hosted on an application server under IIS.

* SQL Server: a dedicated application database, separate from the AX business database.

* AX adapter: communicates with AX through custom AIF services and their supported endpoints.

* SignalR: updates the queue display and operator screens in real time.

* Background worker: retries failed integration calls, refreshes order status, and reconciles operations.

Use the .NET version supported by your chosen hosting environment and deployment standards. The Angular application can evolve independently from the backend.

### 2\. Business workflow

```mermaid

flowchart TD
    A["Commercial plans client for tomorrow"] --> B["Select multiple AX sales orders"]
    B --> C["Client arrives at reception"]
    C --> D["Issue one daily ticket"]
    D --> E["Credit manager processes queue"]
    E --> F{"Payment and credit approved?"}
    F -- No --> G["On hold / rejected with reason"]
    G --> E
    F -- Yes --> H["Release eligible sales orders in AX"]
    H --> I["Warehouse prepares orders"]
    I --> J{"All ticket orders ready?"}
    J -- No --> I
    J -- Yes --> K["Ticket becomes READY"]
    K --> L["Display ticket number on pickup screen"]
    L --> M["Confirm collection"]
```

### Rules to enforce

* A client can have several sales orders under the same pickup plan and ticket.

* A ticket is issued only once when reception confirms arrival.

* Credit processing follows arrival order by default, with authorized hold/skip handling.

* Every order must pass its own eligibility checks before being released in AX.

* The ticket becomes `READY` only when every active order attached to it is ready.

* The reception display shows only the ticket number and pickup instruction, not customer financial information.

* Collection is recorded by an authorized employee.

* Every decision, transition, failure, and retry is logged.

A ticket-level credit approval should not bypass order-level AX restrictions. If an order cannot be released, the ticket remains blocked until the issue is resolved or an authorized employee removes that order under a defined business rule.

