### 5.3 Credit decision contract

```json
{
  "decision": "APPROVED",
  "comment": "Payment verified",
  "orders": [
    {
      "salesId": "SO-001258",
      "approveForRelease": true
    },
    {
      "salesId": "SO-001264",
      "approveForRelease": false
    },
    {
      "salesId": "SO-001275",
      "approveForRelease": true
    }
  ]
}
```

The backend must validate the authenticated user's role, the current ticket state, and each order's current AX eligibility. It then records the decision and requests release only for eligible, approved orders.

A successful API response should distinguish between `APPROVED`, `RELEASE_PENDING`, and `RELEASED`. Do not report an order as released until AX confirms the result.

### 6\. AX 2012 R3 integration

This is the most important part because payment and sales order release must remain consistent with the ERP.

### 6.1 Custom AX services

Create a dedicated AX service group, for example `DOSI_PickupManagementServiceGroup`, containing services for customer/order lookup, credit information, release operations, and warehouse status.

Suggested contracts:

| AX service operation       | Input                      | Output                                       |
| -------------------------- | -------------------------- | -------------------------------------------- |
| GetCustomerForPickup       | Customer account           | Customer identity, active status             |
| GetEligibleSalesOrders     | Customer, warehouse        | Sales orders and eligibility                 |
| GetCustomerCreditInfo      | Customer account           | Balance, credit limit, relevant open items   |
| ValidateOrderForRelease    | Sales order ID             | Eligibility and blocking reasons             |
| ReleaseSalesOrderForPickup | Sales order ID, request ID | Release result, AX reference, error          |
| GetSalesOrderPickupStatus  | Sales order IDs            | Current ERP and warehouse statuses           |
| GetPickupIntegrationStatus | Request ID                 | Operation result and correlation information |

Use serializable AX data contracts or AIF-compatible contract classes, with stable operation names and versioned request/response formats.

### 6.2 Read operations

The .NET API calls AX services to retrieve:

* Customer account and display name.

* Sales orders belonging to the customer.

* Order status and warehouse/site.

* Customer balance and credit limit.

* Any existing AX block or release restriction.

* Warehouse preparation or shipment status, according to your actual AX implementation.

Do not assume that one AX field contains the complete credit decision. Your service must use the same business rules as the existing AX credit control and payment processes.

For customer balance, you may need to reuse your established AX logic for operational and financial balances, rather than implementing a different calculation in the new application.

### 6.3 Sales order release

The credit manager's action should call a custom AX operation that:

* Reloads the sales order and customer in AX.

* Verifies that the order exists and belongs to the expected customer and company.

* Checks the current financial and business restrictions.

* Checks whether the order is already released.

* Executes the appropriate existing AX release logic.

* Returns a structured success or failure result.

* Records the request ID for tracing and duplicate prevention.

Do not implement release as a direct SQL update. Sales order release may involve AX business logic, reservations, warehouse processing, and other controls that must remain intact.

The precise AX class and method used for release must be determined from your existing AX 2012 R3 customization and warehouse setup. A custom service should wrap the correct existing operation rather than bypassing it.

### 6.4 AIF reliability and retries

AX AIF calls may time out even if AX has completed the operation. The application therefore needs an integration workflow:

```mermaid

flowchart TD
    A["Credit manager approves order"] --> B["Save decision and integration request"]
    B --> C["Call AX custom service"]
    C --> D{"Confirmed result?"}
    D -- Yes --> E["Store AX result and update order state"]
    D -- Timeout --> F["Mark status UNKNOWN / RECONCILING"]
    D -- Business error --> G["Record error and require correction"]
    F --> H["Query AX using request ID or order state"]
    H --> I{"Operation completed in AX?"}
    I -- Yes --> E
    I -- No --> J["Retry only when safe"]
```

For reliable integration:

* Persist the request before sending it to AX.

* Use an operation ID that is also recognized or recorded by the AX service.

* Use an outbox/background worker pattern to retry pending requests.

* Query AX to resolve uncertain outcomes before resubmitting a release.

* Keep technical failures separate from business rejections.

* Log request ID, sales order ID, timestamps, and sanitized error details.

* Alert an administrator when reconciliation cannot determine the outcome.

A retry must not create duplicate financial or warehouse transactions.

### 6.5 Warehouse preparation integration

First establish how the warehouse actually processes orders in AX.

If warehouse workers prepare orders in AX, the pickup application should read the real preparation status through a custom AX service or a reliable status synchronization process. The web application can record the pickup-specific workflow, but it must not falsely mark the AX order as prepared.

If the warehouse will update preparation exclusively in the new application, define a supported AX operation for the required ERP changes and validate its impact on inventory, reservations, picking, and shipment processing before implementation.

### 7\. Security and permissions

Use corporate authentication if available; otherwise use ASP.NET Core Identity with secure password storage and role-based authorization.

| Role               | Permissions                                                    |
| ------------------ | -------------------------------------------------------------- |
| Commercial         | Create and edit pickup plans before arrival                    |
| Reception          | Confirm arrivals, issue tickets, view queue                    |
| Credit manager     | View financial data, decide credit, authorize release requests |
| Warehouse operator | View authorized orders, update preparation status              |
| Supervisor         | Monitor all operations, review history, resolve exceptions     |
| Administrator      | Configure users, roles, warehouse, ticket numbering            |
| Display device     | Read only public ticket display state                          |

Additional security requirements:

* Use HTTPS and secure authentication tokens or cookies.

* Validate authorization on every backend endpoint.

* Restrict AX service access to the application server and authorized service identity.

* Keep passwords, AX credentials, and connection strings out of source control.

* Record who approved, released, prepared, and collected each order.

* Avoid exposing sensitive financial data to reception and public display users.

* Apply retention rules to logs and customer-related data.

### 8\. Business exceptions to handle

| Situation                        | Expected behavior                                                     |
| -------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| Client arrives without a plan    | Allow only an authorized walk-in process                              |
| Client arrives twice             | Return the existing active ticket                                     |
| Sales order is cancelled in AX   | Flag it and prevent further release                                   |
| Payment is not validated         | Keep the order blocked                                                |
| AX service is unavailable        | Queue the request or show a pending status; never claim success       |
| Only some orders are ready       | Keep the ticket in preparation                                        |
| One order is blocked             | Keep that order blocked and apply the configured partial-order policy |
| Client never arrives             | Mark the plan absent after the defined cutoff                         |
| Client collects the products     | Record collection time and user                                       |
| Ticket numbering resets next day | Start a new sequence without overwriting historical tickets           |

I recommend one ticket per client, with order-level statuses. That lets the warehouse prepare several orders in parallel while the client receives a single arrival number.

